body:
`MyAvatar` had no default background color (transparent) and OpenPeeps' visual centering offset was set to -5 instead of the correct -6. The debug section in `MyAvatarEditor` also didn't expose locked/blocked props.

## Changes

- **`MyAvatar` — default `backgroundColor`**: Changed from `undefined` → `'#ffffff'`. All avatar instances now render with a white background without requiring an explicit prop (matching what the experimental avatars screen was already passing manually via `AVATAR_BACKGROUND_COLOR`).

- **`MyAvatar` — OpenPeeps `translateX` default**: Changed from `-5` → `-6` for better visual centering of the avatar within its clipping circle.

- **`MyAvatarEditor` debug section — `translateX` display**: Updated `initialValue` and the displayed hint string to reflect the new `-6` default.

- **`MyAvatarEditor` debug section — Blocked Props list**: When `debugMode: true` and `lockedProps` is non-empty, a new **"Blocked Props"** `SettingsList` group renders in the debug panel, showing each locked prop key and its injected value with a lock icon.

```tsx
// Blocked Props section rendered in debug mode when lockedProps is set
// e.g. lockedProps: { [AvatarPropKey.OpenPeeps.SCALE]: '100' }
// → shows: 🔒 scale  |  100
```

See: phcsdm-2NN-p2
Fixes [STRIPE-1116](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/STRIPE-1116)

## Changes proposed in this Pull Request:

When a user manually updates the WooCommerce Stripe plugin from `wp-admin/plugins.php`, the existing **Updated!** notice now gets a "See what's new" link appended to it. The link reuses WordPress' built-in plugin information modal — same logic as the standard "View details" link — and opens it directly on the **Changelog** tab via `&section=changelog`. This gives users immediate access to the release notes for the version they just installed without leaving the Plugins page.

Implementation notes:

- `WC_Stripe_Plugins_Page_Controller` now ensures `thickbox` and `plugin-install` are enqueued on `plugins.php`, and localizes `plugin_slug` + `view_changelog_url` into `wcStripePluginsPageParams`.
- A new `client/entrypoints/plugins-page/append-changelog-link.js` listens for the WP `wp-plugin-update-success` jQuery event. If `response.slug` matches our plugin, it appends the link to the `Updated!` `<p>` inside the matching `tr.plugin-update-tr`. The handler is idempotent and a no-op when jQuery / params are missing.
- Coverage: 6 Jest specs (slug match, ignore other plugins, no double-insert, missing-jQuery / missing-params no-op, listener cleanup) and 2 PHPUnit specs (thickbox enqueued, localized params contract).

